# Hand-derived from X.691 (02/2021) 14.2, 23.6 (the root index a constrained
# whole number, lb 0, ub the root values less one) and 11.5.7.2, 11.5.7.3,
# ALIGNED variant.
#
# 300 root values: two octets, octet-aligned.
accept E300 0000 "e0"
accept! E300 0100 "e256"  # asn1c: 9 bits, see below
accept! E300 012b "e299"  # asn1c: 9 bits, see below
reject! E300 012c  # index 300: no such value; asn1c: 9 bits
reject E300 00  # the second octet missing
# 256 root values: one octet, octet-aligned.
accept E256 00 "f0"
accept E256 ff "f255"
# extensible: the extension bit, then padding, then the root index.
accept! E300x 000005 "e5"  # asn1c: 9 bits, see below
# an extension value: 1, then the normally small index 1, 0 000001 (11.6.1)
accept E300x 81 "x2"
# a CHOICE: the index, then the alternative.
# an extension alternative: 1, the normally small index 0, then its open
# type, octet-aligned, one octet, BOOLEAN TRUE
accept! W 40000102 {"flag": true, "e": "e1", "f": "f2"}  # preamble bit (x absent), flag, padding, E300 e1, E256 f2; asn1c: 9 bits

# asn1c (NativeEnumerated_aper.c, constr_CHOICE_aper.c) aligns an index
# whose upper bound is 255 or more, then reads it in range_bits bits (9 for
# 300 values) instead of 11.5.7.3's two octets; and it does not check the
# padding. From 256 to 511 values its field is 9 bits.
deviates asn1c E300
deviates asn1c E300x
deviates asn1c W

# rasn 0.28 reads and writes an ENUMERATED root index of 256 or more values in
# the bits the range needs, 9 for 300, and not octet-aligned (11.5.7.3: two
# octets, aligned): `0100` it decodes as e2. Its CHOICE index is X.691's.
deviates rasn E300
deviates rasn E300x
deviates rasn W
# ... and rasn's encoder writes an INTEGER (0..256) or wider alternative
# after a two-octet CHOICE index otherwise than X.691 (a256 = 1, `01000001`,
# it sends as `0100000002`); it decodes X.691's. Alone, or after a short
# index, it encodes such an INTEGER right.
